Start with your database:

USE HealtchareDatabse;
GO

Explain:

“Before running our SQL queries, I am selecting the healthcare database that contains our Patient table.”

Part 1 — What is COUNT in SQL?

Start with the simplest query:

SELECT COUNT(*)
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO

Explain very slowly:

SELECT     → I want SQL Server to return something
COUNT      → Count something
(*)        → Count all rows
FROM       → Where should SQL look?
dbo.Patient → Patient table

Simple explanation:

“COUNT(*) tells SQL Server to count how many rows exist in the Patient table.”

You can use a hospital analogy:

“Imagine a hospital database has hundreds or thousands of patients. Instead of scrolling through every patient record and manually counting them, SQL can calculate the total immediately.”

Give the result a meaningful name

Run:

SELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalPatients
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO

Explain AS simply:

“Instead of SQL showing a column with no meaningful heading, I am giving the result a friendly name called TotalPatients.”

Concept:

COUNT(*) AS TotalPatients
           ↑
           Friendly result column name
COUNT a Specific Column

Now run:

SELECT COUNT(CountryOfBirth) AS PatientsWithCountryOfBirth
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO

Explain an important difference:

“COUNT(*) counts rows. But when I give COUNT a particular column, SQL counts the non-NULL values in that column.”

Very simple comparison:

COUNT(*)                 → Counts rows
COUNT(CountryOfBirth)    → Counts non-NULL CountryOfBirth values

Don't go too deeply into NULL yet. Tell viewers you will cover NULL properly later.

Count Unique Values

Since your previous video explained DISTINCT, this is a nice connection:

SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT CountryOfBirth) AS TotalCountries
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO

Explain:

“Previously, we used DISTINCT to display unique countries. Now I can combine COUNT and DISTINCT to find how many different countries exist.”

Breakdown:

COUNT
  ↓
Count

DISTINCT
  ↓
Unique values

CountryOfBirth
  ↓
Column

So:

COUNT(DISTINCT CountryOfBirth)

means:

“Count how many unique countries exist.”

Part 2 — What is TOP in SQL Server?

First remind viewers what this does:

SELECT *
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO

Explain:

“This can return every patient in our Patient table.”

Now introduce:

SELECT TOP (10) *
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO

Explain:

SELECT     → Retrieve data
TOP (10)   → Return only 10 rows
*          → All columns
FROM       → From
dbo.Patient → Patient table

Say:

“TOP does not delete any patients. It only limits how many rows SQL Server returns to us.”

TOP with Specific Columns

This will look much cleaner on your screen:

SELECT TOP (10)
    PatientID,
    FirstName,
    LastName,
    DateOfBirth,
    CountryOfBirth,
    Citizenship
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO

Explain:

“I am combining two things we already learned. I am selecting only the columns I need, and TOP limits the output to ten patients.”

Change TOP 10 to TOP 5

Show beginners that the number is adjustable:

SELECT TOP (5)
    PatientID,
    FirstName,
    LastName,
    CountryOfBirth
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO

Then:

SELECT TOP (20)
    PatientID,
    FirstName,
    LastName,
    CountryOfBirth
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO

Explain:

“TOP is not permanently fixed at 10. I can request TOP 5, TOP 10, TOP 20, TOP 100, depending on what I need.”

Important TOP Concept

At the end, mention one important professional point:

SELECT TOP (10)
    PatientID,
    FirstName,
    LastName
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO

Without sorting, SQL Server is simply being asked for 10 qualifying rows.

So don't tell students:

“TOP 10 means the first 10 patients forever.”

Instead say:

“TOP limits the result to 10 rows. Later, when we learn ORDER BY, we will control exactly which 10 rows we want—for example, newest patients or oldest patients.”

Then you can briefly demonstrate:

SELECT TOP (10)
    PatientID,
    FirstName,
    LastName
FROM dbo.Patient
ORDER BY PatientID;
GO

But don't deeply explain ORDER BY; save it for its own video.

Final Practice Queries
Practice 1 — Total patients
SELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalPatients
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO
Practice 2 — Unique countries
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT CountryOfBirth) AS TotalCountries
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO
Practice 3 — Show only 10 patients
SELECT TOP (10)
    PatientID,
    FirstName,
    LastName,
    DateOfBirth,
    CountryOfBirth,
    Citizenship
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO
Final Explanation

Finish the video with:

“COUNT answers how many? TOP answers how many rows do I want SQL Server to display? COUNT gives me a calculated number, while TOP limits the actual records returned.”

Use this easy comparison:

SQL	Purpose	Example
COUNT(*)	Count all rows	How many patients?
COUNT(Column)	Count non-NULL values	How many patients have CountryOfBirth?
COUNT(DISTINCT Column)	Count unique values	How many different countries?
TOP (5)	Return only 5 rows	Show 5 patients
TOP (10)	Return only 10 rows	Show 10 patients
